# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Introduction
P("The Rhine runs from Switzerland",
  r"The Rhine runs from Switzerland through France, Germany, and the Netherlands to the port of Rotterdam, and its valley forms one of Europe's industrial cores. The four German states it crosses produce about half of Germany's output \citep{vgrdl2025}. Europe's largest chemical site stands on its bank at Ludwigshafen, and its largest steel site at Duisburg. The refineries, power stations, and cement and fertilizer plants of the valley take their feedstock from the river \citep{ccnr2019,icpr2020}. Inland waterways carry only about 7\,\% of German freight, measured in tonne-kilometers, but about a fifth of its dry bulk and a third of its liquid bulk (Supplementary Section~S2).")

P("The summer of 2018 showed",
  r"The summer of 2018 showed what this does to an economy. Kaub stayed below the 78\,cm low-water mark (the level below which the fairway depth is no longer guaranteed) for four months and fell to 25\,cm on 22 October, against a typical summer level of about 1.5\,m. Barge freight rates rose two and a half times on dry cargo and much more on tankers. A steelmaker at Duisburg declared force majeure, the chemical site at Ludwigshafen cut output, petrol stations in the south ran dry, and the federal government released strategic fuel reserves \citep{icpr2020,ccnr2019}. Statistical studies made after the event put the cost at about 1\,\% of German industrial production for each month of low water, which left GDP about 0.4\,\% below its path at the trough of the episode and 0.2--0.3\,\% over its two quarters \citep{ademmer2023,jannsen2026lowwater}. The counts made for the barge sector and the river commission put the direct cost to Germany at EUR~2.4 and 4.7 billion \citep{streng2020laagwater,kriedel2019lowwater}.")

d.sub(r"set a new one on 4 August and fell to 5\,cm on 17 August.", r"set a new one on 4 August, and fell to 5\,cm on 17 August.")

P("A low river is a transport shock",
  r"A low river is a transport shock that propagates: the loss arrives through deliveries that do not come, weeks later and often far from the water. The estimates above are statistical: they correlate the industrial production index with the days of low water of past events \citep{ademmer2023}. They do not say how the losses are generated: which firms lose, how far down the supply chain the loss travels, and which rerouting, stockpiling, or alternative supplier can curb it. Building resilience requires this knowledge. Transport economics evaluates freight prices, loading restrictions, and the shift between rail and road \citep{jonkeren2007,jonkeren2011,jonkeren2014,beuthe2014,christodoulou2020}, not the supply chains behind them. European assessments of drought count direct losses, of which inland navigation is a small share, without following them into the economy \citep{naumann2021drought,cammalleri2020drought}.")

P("Supply-chain models of disasters",
  r"Supply-chain models of disasters track how a shock propagates through the economy by following supply and demand through input--output relationships over time \citep{hallegatte2008,hallegatte2014,otto2017,inoue2019,guan2020}. In Europe, river floods, heatwaves, and the pandemic have been evaluated this way, or with general-equilibrium models, at the resolution of regions and sectors \citep{koks2016,koks2019floods,mendozatinoco2020flood,garcialeon2021heat,pichler2022}. Firm-level models built on observed transaction data have shown how much that aggregation hides \citep{diem2022,diem2024aggregation}. None of them includes a transport network, and in global agent-based models of weather extremes, transport is a delay rather than an actual route that can be disrupted \citep{willner2018floods,kuhla2021resonance}. The models that do represent transport networks, for national infrastructure systems \citep{thacker2017systems,koks2019electricity}, for the world's ports and chokepoints \citep{verschuur2022criticality,verschuur2025chokepoints}, or as capacity limits on trade between European regions \citep{vanniyaperumal2026mria}, do not capture firm-level supply chains. Economic assessments of waterway disruptions in the Mississippi basin, input--output or general-equilibrium, treat the river as a static price shock or a lost flow rather than a logistic link \citep{mackenzie2012,chen2024mississippi,chen2025mississippi}. Our earlier work coupled a firm-level supply-chain model to national road and multimodal networks \citep{colon2021,colon2022disruptsc}. Here we build it for a whole continental economy. To our knowledge, no supply-chain model of this scope exists: one in which every shipment between the firms of \NRegions{} economies is routed by cargo type on a shared multimodal network, and in which the agents that reroute, switch mode, wait, or stop are firms rather than sectors.")

P("We simulate the 2026 low water",
  r"We simulate the 2026 low water with DisruptSC calibrated on 28 European economies (the EU-27 and Switzerland). \NFirms{} firm agents exchange goods with each other, with households, and with external trading partners over \NLinks{} million commercial links, each routed by cargo type on the European road, rail, waterway, and sea network. Each week, the river's capacity is capped by what vessels can load at that week's gauge. Shipments pay a low-water surcharge, reroute if a cheaper path exists, or wait when tank barges and ore trains have no road or rail equivalent. Firms draw on input inventories at the levels of their industry's balance-sheet statistics and stop producing only when an input their industry rates as critical runs out. Every parameter is set from external evidence or calibrated on normal traffic, and one modeling choice, the representation of the river, was made on the 2018 low water (Methods). The model is then run on the 2026 season as observed, before the production statistics of October to December were published. The estimates made during the event extrapolated the 2018 response of industrial production to low-water days \citep{ifw2026,kiel2026autumn,jannsen2026lowwater} or ran a regional model of the corridor's freight and economy \citep{prognos2026rhine}. Ours is, to our knowledge, the first from a model of supply-chain propagation, with a test against statistics that are still to be published (Supplementary Section~S6).")

P("Four results follow.",
  r"Four results follow. First, on the 2018 low water, the model's loss of industrial production lies within the uncertainty band of the statistical estimate. The model predicts an economy-wide loss two and a half times larger, most of it in services that cannot make up for lost activity, which the statistics of the autumn of 2026 will test. Second, shortages, not freight bills, drive the loss: higher freight rates alone cause no loss of production, while doubling inventories reduces the loss by almost three-quarters. Third, timing and profile matter: the same reduction of river capacity spread evenly would cost \FlatLess\,\% less, and the two low-water episodes of 2026 cost \WaveInterShare\,\% more in sequence than apart. Fourth, one more week of inventories or a fairway 20\,cm deeper each removes two-fifths of the loss, and a fleet built for low water more than a fifth. What the railway can take on at short notice removes a quarter to more than a half. The explicit transport network is what makes these levers visible: the model shows where a shortage starts, how far it travels, and which logistic lever curbs it.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Results: the model
P("We built a spatial supply-chain model",
  r"We built a spatial supply-chain model of \NRegions{} European economies at the resolution of \NFirms{} firm agents, 251 regional household agents, and twelve external trading partners, connected by \NLinks{} million commercial links (Fig.~\ref{fig:model}, Methods). Only the inputs that an industry survey rates as critical \citep{pichler2022} can stop production. Firms hold input inventories for a number of days set by their industry's balance-sheet statistics, treat the same product from several regions as one input (pooling), and order weekly from their suppliers. Shipments carry a cargo type (containers, dry bulk, or liquid bulk) and take the cheapest route compatible with it on the European multimodal network. Crude oil and natural gas move by pipeline, outside this network.")

P("The baseline reproduces the national",
  r"The baseline reproduces the national input--output accounts and approximates the modal shares by country and cargo type and the tonnage along the Rhine from Basel to Rotterdam, with residuals that we report (Supplementary Section~S2). The Kaub stretch carries \KaubMt~Mt a year against 50--60 in the counts, of which \KaubContMt~Mt of containers against roughly 10 in reality \citep{ccnr2023}. The Lower Rhine, the Dutch delta, and the Upper Rhine above Mannheim carry less than observed. These residuals do not reduce the loss, since the tonnage at Kaub is above the counts, but they place part of it in the Rhine--Neckar region rather than in Switzerland and Alsace. A low-water week is represented at the Kaub gauge, which sets the share of normal tonnage the stretch can pass that week (the loading curve) and the surcharge on the journey. What exceeds the week's capacity looks for another route and waits when none is acceptable. Bulk switches to rail only when it already travels a main leg of its journey by rail, a rule that sensitivity runs test both ways (Supplementary Sections~S8 and S9).")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Results: the 2018 test
P("We set each mechanism above",
  r"We set each mechanism above to the value given by external evidence (Methods) and test the model on the 2018 low water against the only measurement available: the statistical response of German industrial production to the number of days per month with Kaub below 78\,cm \citep{ademmer2023,ademmer2019}. This is a regression estimate of what production would have been without low water, not an observed series. Applied to 2018, it puts monthly industrial production \RecAug, \RecSep, \RecOct, \RecNov{} and \RecDec\,\% below its no-low-water path from August to December, that is \RecordInt{} percent-months in all, with a one-standard-deviation band of \RecordIntLow--\RecordIntHigh{} percent-months (Methods). On the same indicator, the model gives \IndAug, \IndSep, \IndOct, \IndNov{} and \IndDec\,\%, \ModelInt{} percent-months, with November as the lowest month (Fig.~\ref{fig:validation}a). The GDP effect of \ExPostLow--\ExPostHigh\,\% reported for 2018 \citep{ademmer2019,ademmer2023} is this path's November value times industry's share of the economy. The model's German loss over the whole 2018 event is \EnsMeanyy\,$\pm$\,\EnsSdyy\,\% of a quarter of national value added across the eleven supplier--buyer networks, of which \IndustryShareyy\,\% in industry. This industrial part is \IndQyy\,\% of a quarter, against \RecordQ\,$\pm$\,\RecordQSd\,\% for the benchmark's industry channel. The peak falls between late October and the first week of December (Fig.~\ref{fig:validation}c).")

P(r"\caption{\textbf{The 2018 test.}",
  r"\caption{\textbf{The 2018 test.} (a) Monthly shortfall of German industrial production in 2018: the statistical benchmark with its one-standard-deviation band (green), the model as the mean of the eleven supplier--buyer networks with their range (gray), and the alternative representation of the river, surcharge, and closures, at the same inventories (dashed). (b) The same shortfall summed from August to December, in percent-months, on the reference network against the inventory multiplier, with the eleven networks at $\times$1 (gray), the benchmark (green), the loading curve with its capacity reduction raised and lowered by 15\,\% (blue triangles), and two suppliers per input (cross). (c) Estimated weekly German value-added loss in 2018 (line and bands as in a), with two flags: (i) end-of-October announcements (force majeure at thyssenkrupp, strategic fuel-reserve release), (ii) November as the deepest month. (d) The spread across the eleven reconstructed supply-chain networks for each event, with mean and standard deviation.}")

P("In our baseline representation,",
  r"In our baseline representation, river capacity follows the Kaub gauge through a loading curve built from heterogeneous evidence from barge operators and an inland-navigation association (Methods). Losses start in August, as in the statistical benchmark. With an alternative representation, in which a lower water level only creates a surcharge until each vessel class stops sailing, losses start two and a half months later (Supplementary Section~S5). The balance-sheet inventory level and one supplier per input are not fitted to the benchmark. Across ten further versions of the supplier--buyer network (Methods), November is always the deepest month. The cumulated shortfall is \ModelIntEnsMean\,$\pm$\,\ModelIntEnsSd{} percent-months, and \DrawsInBand{} of the eleven networks fall inside the benchmark's band, the others below it. The loading curve is tested through its capacity reduction, the share of the normal tonnage that it keeps off the river at each gauge: raised and lowered by 15\,\%, it gives \TableLowInt{} and \TableHighInt{} percent-months, both below the benchmark's central value (Fig.~\ref{fig:validation}b, Supplementary Section~S9).")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Results: the 2026 event
P("The 2026 event is made of two",
  r"The 2026 event is made of two low-water episodes (Fig.~\ref{fig:event}a). After the August 2026 record (11\,cm in the week of 10 August), the river recovered to 67\,cm, fell again through September, and at the turn of October reached the gauge zero (a conventional datum above the river bed): the weekly mean was 2\,cm in the week of 28 September, and the daily mean $-5$\,cm on 1 October. It stayed below 25\,cm until late October, rose to 45\,cm in the week of 26 October, and fell back to 18\,cm in the first week of November. The record ends on 10 November. After that, the profile follows the forecasts of the Federal Institute of Hydrology, which show the river above 130\,cm from 16 November (Methods).")

P("In the model, the Kaub reach passes",
  r"In the model, the Kaub reach passes \KaubPassJul\,\% of its normal tonnage in July, \KaubPassAug\,\% in August, \KaubPassSep\,\% in September, and \KaubPassOct\,\% in October, with \KaubMinWeek\,\% in the worst week. The low water costs Germany \EnsMean\,$\pm$\,\EnsSd\,\% of a quarter of value added across the eleven supplier--buyer networks (range \EnsMin--\EnsMax), about \EnsMeanBn~billion USD. This is the loss cumulated over the 43 weeks of the run, divided by thirteen weeks of baseline value added: a size, not the growth of any quarter. The peak falls between the weeks of \PeakDateFirst{} and \PeakDateLast{} in every version. On a reference network, Germany loses \DEUlossQ\,\% (\DEUlossBn~billion USD), \IndQ\,\% of it in industry, with a peak of \DEUpeakWeek\,\% of a week's value added in the week of \DEUpeakDate. The loss builds from July, when the river still passes two to four fifths of its normal tonnage (\EarlyWaveQ\,\% of a quarter by the first days of August), and the August episode costs \AugWaveQ\,\%. The autumn episode costs \AutumnWaveQ\,\%, nearly three times as much, because it is longer and deeper and meets inventories the August episode has lowered.")

P("Several institutes published",
  r"Several institutes published impact estimates for the August 2026 closure. The Kiel Institute \citep{ifw2026} estimated in late July EUR 1--2 billion of industrial loss over five weeks (0.1--0.2 percentage points of third-quarter GDP), and other estimates made in August and September gave 0.2--0.35 points \citep{bloomberg2026commerzbank,kiel2026autumn,jannsen2026lowwater}. Our model gives an industrial loss of \AugWaveIndQ\,\% of a quarter over the five weeks of the August episode, about EUR~\AugWaveEurBn~billion. The share of firms affected is only partly reproduced. In the survey of 29 July--4 August, 33\,\% of the Rhine-corridor firms reported production restrictions and 6\,\% a stop \citep{dihk2026}. In the week that survey closed, \CorridorFirmsBelowRhineAug\,\% of the model's German firms within 50\,km of the Rhine produce below 99\,\% of their baseline and \CorridorStopRhineAug\,\% below half of it. The model is on the low side, which the survey's sample may explain in part (Supplementary Section~S6).")

P("Run apart on the reference network",
  r"Run apart on the reference network (Fig.~\ref{fig:waves}), the first episode, from the descent of June to the partial recovery of late August, costs \WaveAQ\,\% of a quarter and the second, from September to the recovery of November, \WaveBQ\,\%: \WaveSumQ\,\% in all. In sequence, they cost \DEUlossQ\,\%, i.e., \WaveInterShare\,\% more: the second episode arrives when inventories have been drained by the first, and orders are still unfilled (the backlog). After the first episode, the second adds \WaveBafterAQ\,\% instead of the \WaveBQ\,\% it costs alone.")

P("The depth of the episodes counts",
  r"The depth of the episodes counts more than their sequence. The same reduction of river capacity spread evenly over the twenty-three weeks costs \FlatQ\,\%, \FlatLess\,\% less than the season as it happened, because an acute low water empties the yards that a longer, shallower one would keep fed. The memory of the sequence fades as the inventories refill. In counterfactual runs with normal water between the two episodes, the sequence costs \WaveInterOne\,\% more than the sum with one week in between, \WaveInterFour\,\% with four, and \WaveInterEightAbs\,\% less with eight, when the second meets plants working off the first's backlog above baseline. How fast this memory fades depends on the refill time of the inventory rule, 30 days, and on what suppliers and the reopened river can deliver during the recovery (Supplementary Section~S7).")

P(r"\caption{\textbf{The memory of a low-water season.}",
  r"\caption{\textbf{The memory of a low-water season.} (a) Weekly German loss of the 2026 season (black), of its first episode alone (blue, 22 June--30 August), of its second episode alone (green, from 31 August) and of their sum (dashed); the shaded area is what the sequence adds. (b) Cumulated German loss of the two episodes in sequence against the weeks of normal water between them, with the loss of the season as it happened (dash-dotted), the sum of the two episodes alone (dashed), and the flat profile with the same cumulated capacity reduction (dotted). Reference network, not mean across networks.}")

P(r"Households lose \ConsLossEnsBn",
  r"Households lose \ConsLossEnsBn~billion USD of consumption and the 28 modeled economies as a whole \EUlossEnsBn~billion of value added, \NonDEshareEns\,\% of it outside Germany, mostly in France (below). The spread across the eleven networks, \EnsSd\,\% of a quarter around \EnsMean, reflects the sampling uncertainty. The model's rules and parameters move the loss further in both directions, and each is reported separately (Supplementary Fig.~\ref{fig:sensitivity}; Supplementary Sections~S8 and S9).")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Results: reduced capacity
P("Low water has two direct effects",
  r"Low water has two direct effects: some cargo cannot pass, and what passes costs more. We run counterfactuals with one mechanism only. With the surcharge alone and no limit on what the river carries, no production is lost: prices rise, every delivery is made, and the extra freight, about \SurchargeBillBn~billion USD over the season, is passed down the chain in prices. In the model, no buyer cuts its orders when its inputs cost more, and with this level of surcharge no delivery is refused (Methods): freight cost is only about 5\,\% of the input bill of the median firm, and a quarter to a half for transport operators. With the capacity limit alone, the loss is \GateOnlyQ\,\%, even more than with the two together. The surcharge's price signal allocates capacity better: it moves off the river, before it saturates, the shipments that have a rail alternative, and leaves capacity to those that have none. Across all deliveries in the 28 economies, only \PriceShareEconomy\,\% of the value pays more than 1\,\% above its baseline price, because freight is a few percent of the value of most cargo even at the surcharges of the worst weeks (Supplementary Section~S7). Shippers paid several times the normal rate in 2018 and 2026 rather than cancel \citep{ccnr2019,contargo2026}.")

P("The loss is driven by cargo",
  r"The loss is driven by cargo that cannot move: a bulk shipment that exceeds the week's capacity is blocked while its buyer draws down its inventory. In the autumn episode, about \ResentShare\,\% of the cut tonnage is rerouted. Shipments that already travel a main leg by rail go fully by rail. We have no data on the share of the Kaub traffic that combines rail and barge, so this is an assumption of the routing. Without it, the 2026 loss is \RevNoRail\,\% of a quarter, \RevNoRailAbs\,\% more, and the 2018 loss \RevNoRailyy\,\%, \RevNoRailyyAbs\,\% more (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:revision}).")

P("Overall, in the worst week",
  r"Overall, in the worst week, firms that normally ship over Kaub deliver \KaubDeliveredDry\,\% (dry bulk) and \KaubDeliveredWet\,\% (liquid bulk) of their baseline tonnage. The deliveries withheld at the river are less than a third of the deliveries that German buyers miss over the event. The rest is rationing along the chains that start there (Supplementary Section~S7).")

P("The total loss therefore depends",
  r"The total loss therefore depends on how long inventories last: doubling them for every buyer divides the 2018 loss by more than three (\LadderD{} instead of \DEUlossQyy\,\% of a quarter on the reference network), and one extra week removes two-fifths of the 2026 loss. Pooling matters as much: without it, when each firm's input from each region is a specific, non-substitutable input, the loss is \NoPoolQ\,\% of a quarter (2018: \NoPoolQyy\,\% instead of \DEUlossQyy). Rail could take over part of the bulk if wagons were added (Adaptation measures, below).")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Results: who loses
P("Most of the loss lies outside",
  r"Most of the loss lies outside the Rhine industrial zones. On the reference network, of the German gross loss, industry (mining, manufacturing, and utilities) carries \IndustryShare\,\% (of which the utilities \ShareUtilities), construction \ShareConstruction\,\%, agriculture \ShareAgri\,\%, and the services \ShareServices\,\%, the largest group. Two supply chains carry the shock beyond industry: fuel and building materials.")

P(r"\paragraph{The fuel chain}",
  r"\paragraph{The fuel chain} The refineries on the Rhine, at Karlsruhe and in the Cologne area, deliver less, and the sectors that run on their fuel and serve each other lower their output: warehousing and logistics (\ShareLogistics\,\%), land transport (\ShareLandTransport\,\%), electricity and gas (\SharePower\,\%), and water transport (\ShareShipping\,\%) (Supplementary Section~S7). This share rests on two assumptions that we test in Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:revision}: a 90-day coping duration for the service inputs (at 45 days the loss rises by \RevServRel\,\%) and the power sector's fuel-oil input being critical (non-critical, the loss falls by \RevDfuelAbs\,\%).")

P(r"\paragraph{The building-materials chain}",
  r"\paragraph{The building-materials chain} Sand, gravel, cement, and steel move by barge, and construction and the cement industry (\ShareCement\,\%) lose output when their stockyards are empty. Chemicals carry \ShareChemicals\,\% of the loss, hospitality \ShareHospitality\,\%, and refining \ShareRefining\,\%. By the accounting convention of Methods, \PerishableShare\,\% of the German gross loss falls in sectors whose lost weeks are lost for good, utilities and services. Goods producers and construction work off nearly a quarter of their backlog by the end of the run, leaving a net loss of \DEUnetBn~billion USD. The 2018 run has the same anatomy.")

P("Outside Germany, France loses",
  r"Outside Germany, France loses \FRAlossBn~billion USD, two-thirds in its food chain and a sixth in road transport. Farm products of eastern France travel by barge down the Rhine to the French food industry and are withheld at Kaub. Haulers run short of fuel, which comes in part from German refineries, and the food industry rations its clients. The cost-based routing sends this domestic grain by the Rhine, which likely overstates the real barge share, so France's loss reads as an upper bound. Spain, Austria, and the Netherlands lose \ESPlossBn, \AUTlossBn, and \NLDlossBn~billion USD, in farming, in land transport and logistics, and in refinery and oil-and-gas output whose customers upriver cannot be served. Fig.~\ref{fig:who} maps the peak week along the Rhine and Neckar at inland refineries and chemical sites, and across the country in transport, power, and construction, and gives the cumulated loss by country and sector.")

d.sub(r"(the week of \DEUpeakDate 2026)", r"(the week of \DEUpeakDate{} 2026)")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Results: adaptation
P("We rerun the event under five",
  r"We rerun the event under five adaptation scenarios for each of the eleven supplier--buyer networks (Fig.~\ref{fig:levers}). One extra week of inventories for every buyer in the 28 economies removes \LevStockAvg\,\% of the German loss on average (\LevStockRange\,\% across the networks). The same extra week for the barge-dependent buyers alone (transport operators, utilities, chemicals, cement, and basic metals) removes \LevStockTAvg\,\% (\LevStockTRange). Seven days of inputs for every German buyer is about 26 billion USD of goods, which avoids about \StockAvoidedBn~billion of loss in this event. The same extra week for the barge-dependent buyers alone is 4.5 billion of inventory for \StockTAvoidedBn~billion avoided, more than half the effect for a sixth of the inventory.")

d.sub("Applied to the Kaub stretch alone it removes", "Applied to the Kaub stretch alone, it removes")

P("A fleet built for low water removes",
  r"A fleet built for low water removes \LevFleetAvg\,\% (\LevFleetRange) of the loss. After 2018, chemical companies built nine shallow-draft tankers that can still carry about 800 tonnes at a gauge of 30\,cm, compared with about 400 for a standard vessel at the same gauge \citep{wiwo2026,contargo2026}. Generalized to the whole bulk fleet, this lets a further 10\,\% of the normal tonnage pass between 15 and 55\,cm, but below 15\,cm even a low-water vessel loads almost nothing. The cumulated loss falls on every network, yet the worst week rises on \FleetPeakUpDraws{} of the ten further ones (Supplementary Section~S8). With its gain extended to 100\,cm, as the loading of the purpose-built tankers at that gauge suggests, it removes \RevFleetWideRemoves\,\% (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:revision}).")

P("The three together remove",
  r"The three together remove \LevPackageAvg\,\% (\LevPackageRange). The ranking holds across the eleven supplier--buyer networks: inventories or the fairway come first in every one (inventories ahead in \StocksBeatFairwayDraws{} of ten), and the fleet comes last in \FleetLastDraws{} of ten. It is the same for the 28 economies as a whole (on the reference network: inventories \LevEUStock\,\%, fairway \LevEUDeep\,\%, targeted inventories \LevEUStockT\,\%, fleet \LevEUFleet\,\%, the three together \LevEUPackage\,\%).")

P("We test the effect of rail",
  r"We test the effect of rail using two capacity levels for bulk-barge-to-wagon transfer. In the main runs, bulk does not switch, except for shipments whose normal route already includes a main leg by rail. We then allow any disrupted bulk shipment to go to rail, up to 50 kt a week, a ceiling calibrated on the 400 wagons that the national rail operator could add at short notice in August 2026 at two rotations a week \citep{dbcargo2026}. In this configuration, the loss falls by \RevRailFiftyAbs\,\%. With a higher ceiling of 140 kt a week, the response of all rail freight to past low waters estimated by ref.~\citep{ademmer2023}, the loss falls by \RevRailHundredFortyAbs\,\% (Supplementary Section~S8).")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Discussion
P("An intermittent low river",
  r"An intermittent low river is mostly an inventory problem. In the model, higher freight rates alone cause no loss of production, only higher freight bills. Records of 2018 and 2026, when firms paid rates several times higher rather than cancel shipments, support this price-taking behavior. The surcharge is a transfer to carriers that the model prices but does not turn into lost output. The real resources that extra journeys, fuel, and delays consume, and the weight of repeated seasons on the valley's competitiveness, are outside its scope. What stops a plant is a tank or an ore yard that runs empty when the river passes only a fraction of its tonnage week after week. The cost of the event is set by how long the inventories of a buyer, and of its clients, last: doubling them divides the 2018 loss by more than three, and one more week of them removes two-fifths of the 2026 loss. This sharpens a lesson of the disaster literature: inputs are hard to replace in the short run \citep{boehm2019,carvalho2021}, so inventories set how long a shock takes to affect production \citep{hallegatte2014,inoue2019}. In a transport shock with bounded short-term alternatives, here rail, inventories represent most of the response. A firm-level study of the Rhine's bulk buyers \citep{scholten2014} reached a similar conclusion from the other side, measuring the steelworks' vulnerability to low water in days of empty storage and their adaptation in storage capacity to be added.")

P("The comparison with 2018 is",
  r"The comparison with 2018 is a selection and a consistency check, not a validation. The costs of rail and road transport were calibrated on normal traffic. The inventories, the loading curve, and the list of critical inputs come from outside evidence, some of it from 2026 itself. One choice, the representation of the river, was made on the 2018 benchmark, which is itself a statistical estimate with a wide uncertainty band. The model's cumulated loss lies within its 95\,\% band but below its center. The comparison clearly supported one representation of the impact of low water: low water is not a price with a cliff at the depth where vessels stop sailing, but a tonnage constraint that tightens as the gauge falls, best represented by a loading curve. Its effect on industrial production starts in the first weeks of lighter loads, as the 2018 production series shows.")

P("The published estimates focus",
  r"The published estimates focus on industry only and call themselves a lower bound for that reason. The model predicts an economy-wide loss two and a half times larger. This amplification reflects how the model propagates shortages downstream into services, which no statistic yet measures. Input--output models of European floods also place much of the indirect loss in services \citep{mendozatinoco2020flood,koks2019floods}. Here the share follows from one rule: services are not stored and are mostly not made up."
  "\n\n"
  r"The 2026 results are a forecast that readers can check. The model's monthly path of German industrial production from July to December 2026 was fixed before the statistics of October to December were published (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:prospective}). As the production indices are released, they can be compared with this path under a scoring rule set in advance (Supplementary Section~S6): the model fails if it does no better than a forecast of no loss. The turnover indices of the services will test the rule on services in the same way. One gap is already known: the model puts fewer firms in difficulty than the business survey of early August 2026 found (Results).")

P("Statistical studies count the months",
  r"Statistical studies count the months of low water but not their order, so they cannot evaluate a particular profile of troughs and recoveries. Run apart, the two episodes of 2026 cost less than in sequence, and the second costs a fifth more than it would have alone, because it meets the inventories the first one drained. Global models find the same amplification when extremes follow each other closely or strike an economy already under stress \citep{kuhla2021resonance,middelanis2023stressed}. Here it has one carrier, the inventories the first episode leaves behind, and its memory is short: with the 30-day refill time of the model, four weeks of normal water between the episodes reduce the interaction to \WaveInterFour\,\% (Fig.~\ref{fig:waves}b), and a faster refill shortens it further (Supplementary Section~S7). The six-week outlooks of the Federal Institute of Hydrology \citep{bfg2026}, which in September 2026 correctly predicted that the river would not recover before October, are the information on which inventory rules and reserve releases should act. In the paper, inventories are already built before the season, and a rule that raises inventories on the first outlook was not run.")

P("The loss is not borne only",
  r"The loss is not borne only where the river is: industry carries more than a third of it; the fuel chain, from the refineries on the Rhine to haulers, warehouses, power plants, and shipping companies, over half; and the building-materials chain, a sixth. Undelivered services are mostly lost, whereas the plants that stop make up nearly a quarter of their backlog once the river reopens. Two modeling choices shape this picture. First, crude oil and natural gas reach the refineries by pipeline, so no shortage arises there, which matches the record. Second, pooling---the same product from several regions is the same input---divides the loss by about three and a half compared with a model in which every input is specific to its region of origin. Without a more detailed distribution network for fuel or building materials, pooling those inputs spreads the losses across the country, whereas the 2018 record shows them concentrated in the south.")

P("The ranking of adaptations",
  r"The ranking of adaptations is the practical result. One extra week of inventories, held before the season, and a fairway 20\,cm deeper each remove two-fifths of the loss. The same week held by the barge-dependent buyers alone removes a quarter, and a fleet built for low water more than a fifth. The three together remove more than four-fifths. Inventories or the fairway come first on every version of the supplier--buyer network. Rail is a lever whose size is bounded by the number of trains that can be added at short notice. It can decrease the loss by a quarter with a 50-kt barge-to-rail capacity---a ceiling calibrated on what the operator offered in August 2026. Inventories or the fairway still come first under this ceiling, although the benefits then overlap (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:cross}). The one other quantified rail result for low-water events, on the Upper Mississippi, points the same way \citep{chen2024mississippi}. These compare efficacy at stated sizes for one event, not investments: holding costs, perishability, and hazard frequency are outside this paper. On the ground, the fleet lever is taking shape: chemical companies have already put nine low-water tankers into service since 2018, and seven more are planned \citep{wiwo2026}. Deepening the Middle Rhine fairway is planned but will take a decade to materialize. Inventories remain the lever firms can pull before a season.")

P("Four limitations bound",
  r"Four limitations bound our analysis. (i) The loading curve that turns a gauge into capacity is the most sensitive parameter. It is built on loading rules and on the operators' counts of two events (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:loading}). Raising or lowering the share of the normal tonnage it keeps off the river by 15\,\% moves the 2026 loss between \TableHighQ{} and \TableLowQ\,\% of a quarter. (ii) The substitutes are set by rule: containers find rail and road without a limit; bulk may not switch, except for the shipments that already use rail for a main leg of their journey, an assumption that weighs heavily on the result. Making this switch impossible increases the 2026 loss by \RevNoRailAbs\,\%. In 2018, some German importers that used inland waterways shifted to rail and road and kept the new mode \citep{meuchelboeck2025lowwater}, a lasting shift the model does not represent. (iii) The supplier--buyer network is one of many consistent with the national accounts, and the spread across its eleven versions is as wide as the effect of the strongest adaptation. (iv) The transport impact of low water is the only channel represented. Cooling-water and agricultural losses from the same drought are outside the scope.")

P(r"\todo{Overall, I find many sentence", None)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Methods
P("DisruptSC is a spatial agent-based model",
  r"DisruptSC is a spatial agent-based model of supply chains in which firms, households, and external trading partners exchange goods over a transport network \citep{colon2021}. The version used here (v2) adds cargo classes, a multimodal network with mode-specific costs, a partially-binding production function, and input pooling across regions. The code is open.")

P("Eleven sectors are placed",
  r"Eleven sectors are placed on facility data (crops, coal, oil and gas, metal ores, refineries, chemicals, pulp and paper, aluminum, cement, steel, and power, on the production grid, trackers, and facility databases of refs.~\citep{spam2020,gem2025,climatetrace2024,fineprint2022}). The other 39 sectors are placed on NUTS3 employment from the Eurostat regional accounts \citep{eurostat2025regio} (Supplementary Section~S1 gives the dataset of each sector).")

P("Households are one agent per NUTS2",
  r"Households are one agent per NUTS2 region (251), and external partners are one agent at their main logistic entry point on the transport network. Commercial links are drawn from the input--output coefficients: every buyer selects suppliers of each input in each supplying region in proportion to their size, which gives \NLinks{} million links, of which \NRoutable{} carry goods that move on the transport network. Services and same-node exchanges do not use transport infrastructure.")

P("This supplier selection is",
  r"This supplier selection is a seeded random draw with gravity logic: firms buy more from large, nearby suppliers. The eleven versions of the supplier--buyer network vary the seed. Headline numbers are means over the eleven versions. The decompositions by sector, country, and shipment, which need the link-level export, are on the reference network. This is the model's default seed, fixed before any run of this study, which sits below the median of both events. A supplier's size is its share of the output of its region--sector, allocated by employment or production capacity. The number of suppliers drawn per supplying region--sector is a parameter, one in the reference runs; a run with two is reported as a sensitivity (Supplementary Section~S9).")

P(r"\paragraph{Production and propagation.}",
  r"\paragraph{Production and propagation.} Firms produce with the partially-binding Leontief technology of \citet{pichler2022}: an input that is short constrains output only if the industry survey made by \citet{pichler2022} rates it critical (39\,\% of input links by count, 21\,\% important, 40\,\% non-critical; Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:criticality}). In addition, an input representing less than 2\,\% of the buyer's input costs never constrains production, a floor without which negligible cross-border service inputs cascade. The same product bought from several regions is one input: inventories and coefficients are pooled, and orders are split across all suppliers by their baseline share, adjusted by the share of its order that each supplier delivered. In all, 18 products are poolable, standardized goods such as fuels, grain, cement, steel, and basic chemicals (listed in Supplementary Section~S1). The run without pooling bounds this choice.")

P("Firms hold inventories of goods inputs",
  r"Firms hold inventories of goods inputs for a target duration set by their buying industry. The durations come from the raw-material inventories of the Bundesbank corporate balance-sheet statistics \citep{bundesbank2025} (raw materials, consumables and supplies over material costs, 18--70 days across manufacturing industries, 7 days for transport operators) and from physical stock data for bulk feedstocks (coal at power plants 30 days, ore and coking coal at steelworks 40, liquid feedstocks at chemical plants 14). Crude, which reaches the refineries by pipeline, carries the 90 days of the stockholding obligation \citep{ebv2025}. The sources are listed in Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:stocks}.")

P("Service and construction inputs",
  r"Service and construction inputs are not storable and carry a 90-day coping duration, which captures contracts, substitutes, and postponement. Formally, it is an inventory: a buyer that misses a critical service, for instance, the transport services a factory buys, keeps producing at its baseline rate for 90 days. After a shortfall, it is refilled as the late delivery of the service's backlog, which the loss accounting nevertheless counts as lost (Loss accounting). Multiples of these targets are tested in Supplementary Section~S9. Households buy from the same suppliers as firms and lose consumption when suppliers ration.")

P(r"\paragraph{The weekly sequence.}",
  r"\paragraph{The weekly sequence.} Each week, a firm's production target is the sum of its clients' orders of the week before. For each pooled input, it orders what the target requires plus the gap between its inventory and its target, which the firm aims to close over a restoration time $\tau$ of 30 days. The order is split among the suppliers of the pool by their baseline shares, re-weighted by a moving average of the share of past orders each supplier delivered. Production is the least of the target, the capacity (baseline output divided by a utilization rate $u=0.8$, that is 125\,\% of the baseline), and what the inventory of each critical input allows, with a looser bound from the important inputs. A supplier delivers from its production and finished-goods stock and rations clients proportionally. Shipments are then routed (Disruption and response). A shipment turned away at the river, or without an acceptable route, returns to the supplier's output inventory. The delivered price is the supplier's price times one plus the relative change in its freight cost, weighted by the supplier's transport intensity (its transport purchases over its intermediate purchases, 0 to 0.6 across agents). A delivery is refused when this weighted increase exceeds 5, which never happens in this paper's runs. A firm passes any increase in its unit input cost into its own price, so a surcharge propagates down the chain. The equations and the implementation conventions are in Supplementary Section~S4.")

P(r"\paragraph{Transport layer.}",
  r"\paragraph{Transport layer.} The network is the trans-European transport network (TEN-T) with \NEdges{} edges and \NNodes{} nodes of road, rail, inland waterway (the Rhine from Basel to Rotterdam included), sea lanes, and multimodal connectors at terminals and ports (counts by mode in Supplementary Section~S1). Firms attach to road nodes. The supplying sector sets the cargo class: containers for manufactured goods, dry bulk for ores, coal, grain, and building materials, liquid bulk for fuels and basic chemicals. Cost per tonne-kilometer is set per mode and cargo class: on the waterways from operator, vessel-type, and quoted tanker and container rates, on rail and road from the calibration of the modal split, with a value of time per cargo class and transfer costs at terminals (sources in Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:rates}).")

P(r"The Kaub reach carries \KaubMt~Mt a year in the model",
  r"The Kaub reach carries \KaubMt~Mt a year in the model (Results; Supplementary Fig.~S3), of which \KaubContMt~Mt of containers (\KaubContShare\,\%), \KaubDryMt~Mt of dry bulk, and \KaubLiquidMt~Mt of liquid bulk: three fleets on the same river, for which the capacities below are set per class. Capacities are set on the Rhine waterways only. Rail, road, and ports are unconstrained.")

P(r"\paragraph{Disruption and response.}",
  r"\paragraph{Disruption and response.} A low-water week is represented at the Kaub gauge. A loading table converts the weekly mean gauge into the loading share, the share of its normal tonnage that the fleet can carry, from full loading above 150\,cm to a tenth at record lows. It is built from vessel loading rules, single-vessel loads, operators' thresholds, and the one weekly tonnage count for August 2026 \citep{vandorsser2020,contargo2026,bfg2026} (each source in Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:loading}). The loading share acts in two ways. It is a capacity limit: in that week, the Kaub reach can carry, for each cargo class, the loading share times that class's normal tonnage, which is the model's baseline flow of the class on the reach plus a 2\,\% headroom margin (Supplementary Section~S2). The three classes are three fleets, so the room that the container vessels leave when their shipments quit the river at a surcharge is not available to the bulk, and a week at a given gauge rations the same fraction of each class of the model's traffic as of the observed one. The weekly demand and deliveries at Kaub are reported in Supplementary Section~S7, and the headroom is tested at 10 and 25\,\% (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:revision}). The inverse of the loading share is also the journey's cost multiplier, the low-water surcharge, on every stretch upstream from Koblenz. On the Lower Rhine, where vessels load deeper, the multiplier is reduced to one third of its excess over one, as the observed rate schedules show \citep{contargo2026}.")

P("Within a week, every firm first",
  r"Within a week, every firm first sends its shipment on its normal route, or on another one if the surcharge makes it cheaper, including the cost of switching mode. Where a class on a stretch exceeds its capacity, all its shipments through it are cut in proportion so that it is exactly full, and the remainders are assigned to the cheapest route avoiding the saturated reach. A switch of mode is an alternative route that adds more than 50\,km on a mode that carried less than 100\,km of the normal route; for bulk, road always counts as such a mode (Supplementary Section~S5). For containers, the penalty of a switch is 15\,\% of the normal freight bill. For dry and liquid bulk, it is prohibitive, because tank barges and ore trains have no road or rail equivalent at volume in the time of an event, an assumption relaxed in Supplementary Sections~S5 and S8. No water level closes the river: a week at 11\,cm passes a fifth of the normal tonnage, and a week below 5\,cm, 8\,\%. The alternative representation, in which low water acts only through the surcharge and each vessel class stops sailing below its own water level, is documented with its results in Supplementary Section~S5.")

P(r"\paragraph{Scenarios.}",
  r"\paragraph{Scenarios.} The 2026 weekly profile of the Kaub gauge (23 weeks from 22 June, followed by 20 weeks of recovery) is the river as observed: the weekly mean of the PEGELONLINE record of the German waterway administration \citep{pegelonline2026} for the 20 weeks from 22 June to 8 November 2026. The last three weeks rest in part on the forecasts of the Federal Institute of Hydrology (BfG). The week of 9 November takes two observed days and, for the five others, the daily medians of the 14-day forecast of 11 November \citep{bfg2026fourteen}. The week of 16 November takes the same forecast, and the week of 23 November the median of the six-week outlook of 9 November \citep{bfg2026}. These forecasts have the river above 130\,cm from 16 November (weeks flagged in Fig.~\ref{fig:event}). The model, its parameters, and the loading curve were fixed on 2 November 2026. The production indices of July, August, and September 2026, released on 7 September, 7 October, and 6 November, entered no choice.")

P(r"\paragraph{Loss accounting.}",
  r"\paragraph{Loss accounting.} A firm's loss in a week is its production shortfall against its own baseline, weighted by the value-added share of its sector. Losses are expressed as a share of a quarter (13 weeks). This gross loss counts every lost week. In agriculture, mining, manufacturing, and construction, post-shock activity can offset it. We therefore track each firm's backlog, the output it still owes its clients, $B_t=\max(0,B_{t-1}+s_t)$, where $s_t$ is the deviation of production from the baseline, positive for a shortfall, and report the backlog left at the end of the run as net loss. For every other sector (utilities, trade, transport, hospitality, and other services), a lost week is lost, so net equals gross. This classification is an accounting convention, not a prediction of which firms catch up.")

P("The runs are 43 weeks long",
  r"The runs are 43 weeks long for 2026 and 42 for 2018, i.e., the profiles' length plus 20 weeks of recovery, long enough for the accounting to settle (the catch-up that a longer run would add is about \CatchUpTailShare\,\% of the backlog left at the end; Supplementary Section~S4). Aggregates over the 28 economies are sums over the modeled economies. Value-added loss and household consumption loss are two views of the same loss, kept separate, and consumption loss is kept gross.")

P(r"\paragraph{Calibration, selection and validation.}",
  r"\paragraph{Calibration, selection, and validation.} The rail and road costs per tonne-kilometer were set so that the baseline modal split of Germany approaches the Eurostat shares (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:rates} compares the resulting costs with the rate evidence). Nothing else was fitted to the events. One modeling choice was made on the 2018 benchmark: the representation of the river, between two options (Supplementary Section~S5). The level of the inventories and the number of suppliers per input were also compared with the benchmark. Both stay at their reference values, the balance-sheet level and one supplier per input, and the comparisons are reported as sensitivities (Supplementary Section~S9). The 2018 comparison as a whole is therefore an in-sample check of consistency, not a validation. The economy is the 2022 accounts for both events, in 2022 prices and US dollars: the 2018 test uses the river of 2018 on the economy of 2022, assuming that the same sectors depend on the river in similar ways, not as a reconstruction of 2018.")

P("The benchmark is the path of German",
  r"The benchmark is the path of German industrial production that the published regression of ref.~\citep{ademmer2023} implies for the low-water days of 2018, cumulated into a monthly shortfall, with a Monte Carlo band over its coefficients at their published standard errors. The model's counterpart is the shortfall of German industrial production aggregated as the production index, each sector's shortfall rate weighted by its German value added over sections B to D, by calendar month. Supplementary Section~S5 gives the coefficients, the band, the aggregation, and the comparison for the further networks, the loading-curve variants, and two suppliers per input.")

P(r"\paragraph{Adaptation and counterfactuals.}",
  r"\paragraph{Adaptation and counterfactuals.} Each adaptation scenario applies one change to the 2026 run. \textit{Fairway +20\,cm} adds 20\,cm to the gauge on every stretch of the Rhine, for the capacity of the Kaub stretch and for the surcharges of all stretches, a simple proxy of the deepening project (the Abladeoptimierung Mittelrhein: the fairway between Budenheim and St.~Goar from 1.90 to 2.10\,m at the equivalent low-water level, in the federal transport plan since 2016 \citep{bmv2019,baw2026}). A local variant adds the 20\,cm on the Kaub stretch only (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:revision}).")

P(r"The \textit{low-water fleet} raises",
  r"The \textit{low-water fleet} raises the loading curve by 10 percentage points of normal cargo between 15 and 55\,cm, the loading that the nine shallow-draft tankers in service in August 2026 achieve relative to standard vessels, applied to the whole bulk fleet \citep{wiwo2026,contargo2026}. The construction of the curve, its variant extended to 100\,cm, and what the lever holds fixed are in Supplementary Section~S8.")

P("The inventory held under the",
  r"The inventory held under the \textit{inventories +7 days} lever is 7/365 of the buyers' annual purchases of goods inputs (Results gives the amounts). Every adaptation is run on the reference network and, paired with its own base, on the ten further networks. The loss avoided is reported as the mean over the eleven, with their range. The channel decomposition runs the 2026 profile with the surcharge alone (no tonnage limit) and with the capacity limit alone (no surcharge). The sensitivity runs and the two crossed experiments (the single levers on top of the 50 kt rail ceiling; the two episodes at a refill time of 15 days) are listed in Supplementary Sections~S7 to S9. The experiment on the two episodes splits the 2026 profile after its tenth week (30 August). The two parts are run alone, in sequence with 1, 2, 4, and 8 weeks of normal water (200\,cm at Kaub) between them, and as a flat profile at the mean loading share of the season (0.40) for its twenty-three weeks. The interaction is the loss of the sequence minus the sum of the losses of the two episodes alone.")

d.sub(r"\texttt{additional\_data/\allowbreak runs\_20261102.csv}", r"\texttt{additional\_data/\allowbreak runs\_20261111.csv}")

P(r"\paragraph{Prospective test.}",
  r"\paragraph{Prospective test.} The 2026 results are a forecast: they were computed before the production statistics of October to December 2026 were published, so these statistics can prove them wrong. No score is reported in this version, because these statistics were not yet available. Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:prospective} gives the model's monthly shortfall of German industrial production from July to December 2026, and Supplementary Section~S6 the scoring rule, fixed on 9 October 2026. The model's path is compared, month by month, with the Destatis production index of industry for September to December 2026. Its mean absolute error is compared with that of two alternatives: no loss at all, and the path that the published regression \citep{ademmer2023} implies for the same low-water days. The model is judged wrong if its error exceeds that of the no-loss path. The index also carries demand, energy prices, and the industrial trend, so the score measures forecast performance and does not validate the causal loss, which no single realization can. The monthly path, the gauge profile, and the run tables are deposited on Zenodo on the day of submission (\todo{deposit them and write the DOI here}), which gives the forecast a public date.")

# the table of the prospective test leaves the main text (N09): it is Table S-prospective of Section S6
m = re.search(r"\n\\begin\{table\}\[tbp\]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption\{\\textbf\{The prospective test\.\}.*?\n\\end\{table\}\n", d.text, re.DOTALL)
if m and "generated table prospective" in m.group(0):
    d.text = d.text[:m.start()] + d.text[m.end():]
    d.done += 1
else:
    d.fails.append("the table float of the prospective test was not found")

